
Unit testing in Java is the practice of testing the smallest individual parts of an application—typically single methods or classes—in isolation to 
verify they behave as expected. The industry-standard framework for writing and running these automated tests is JUnit.

While JUnit provides the core test runner and structure, developers often pair it with specialized libraries.
> JUnit. The de facto testing framework for Java. JUnit 5 (Jupiter) and JUnit 6 provide modern annotations and test execution engines.
> Mockito. The standard mocking framework used to replace external dependencies (like databases or APIs) with simulated objects so you can test a class in isolation.
> AssertJ. A fluent assertion library that offers more readable, expressive checks than standard JUnit assertions.
> TestNG. An alternative testing framework to JUnit, often used for complex test configurations and parallel execution.
